<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tianshui</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tianshui is a city in south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gansu">Gansu</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, in the valley of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wei-river">Wei River</a>. It lies close to the region where the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/state-of-qin">State of Qin</a> had its origins, and was later a frontier prefecture on the roads to the west, briefly the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xue-ju">Xue Ju</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a> state in 617–618. Nearby are the Buddhist grottoes of Maijishan, carved from about the 5th century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tianshui.jpg" alt="Tianshui" width="320" height="214" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: shizhao · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E4%BC%8F%E7%BE%B2%E5%BA%99%E7%89%8C%E5%9D%8A.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Tianshui is a prefecture-level city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gansu">Gansu</a> province and the second largest in the province after the capital, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lanzhou">Lanzhou</a>. It lies in the valley of the Jie River, a major tributary of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wei-river">Wei River</a>, on the boundary between the Loess Plateau and the Qinling Mountains. The climate is cool and semi-arid, with four seasons of comparatively equal length, cold dry winters and warm, somewhat humid summers; most rain falls from June to September. According to a legend, the name Tianshui, meaning heavenly water, comes from a lake formed from heaven that stayed the same size all year round.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The House of Ying, the ruling family of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a>, the founding dynasty of imperial <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, developed from Quanqiu, present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-county-gansu">Lixian</a>, to the south. After the invasions of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rong-people">Rong</a> that unseated the Western Zhou, Qin recovered the territory of Tianshui from the nomads, and it became an important region of their duchy and, later, kingdom. Characteristic Qin tombs have been excavated at nearby Fangmatan, including a 2,200-year-old map of Qin's Gui County.</p><p>Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin Empire</a> the area belonged to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/longxi-commandery">Longxi Commandery</a>. Emperor Wu of the Han separated it as the Tianshui Commandery in 114 BCE, as part of his expansion towards the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarim-basin">Tarim Basin</a>, and the general Li Guang came from the city. Han conquests and exploration led to the development of the Northern Silk Road, on which Tianshui formed a junction with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wei-river">Wei River</a> before the road passed Mount Long to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/changan">Chang'an</a>, present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xian">Xi'an</a>. Monks travelling along the road and local Buddhists carved the Maijishan Grottoes nearby, which hold thousands of Buddhist sculptures made between the Wei and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> dynasties.</p><p>The city was called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seoul">Hanyang</a> under the Northern Wei, when it was the centre of the Hanyang Commandery, and under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-wei">Western Wei</a> the name was changed to Hanyang County. During the Tang and the Five Dynasties it was known as Shanggui and alternated with Chengji, present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a>'an, as the capital of Qinzhou province. In 1473, under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ming-empire">Ming dynasty</a>, Li County was separated from its jurisdiction.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The 2020 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> counted 2,984,659 inhabitants in the prefecture, of whom 1,212,791 lived in the built-up area formed by the two urban districts of Qinzhou and Maiji. The mild climate makes Tianshui a large producer of fruit, in particular apples, and it is also a major industrial centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gansu">Gansu</a>, especially in electronics. Its best-known dish is guagua, a boiled buckwheat flour dish eaten at breakfast, which legend links to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-dynasty">Han dynasty</a> general Wei Xiao. Besides the Maijishan Grottoes, visitors go to the Fuxi Temple and the Dadiwan Site.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Tianshui” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>He took the title Hegemon-King of Western Qin, then proclaimed himself emperor of Qin and moved his capital east to <b>Tianshui</b>, threatening the Wei valley around Chang'an.</blockquote><a href="/state/qin-xue-ju">Qin (Xue Ju)</a></li></ul></section>
